WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.400
Did "Wonka" show you just a single world of&nbsp;
pure imagination or is there more to come for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:11.080
the young chocolatier and his tiny orange friend?&nbsp;
Here's what the ending of "Wonka" really means.

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:15.680
Willy Wonka is an immigrant fresh off the boat in&nbsp;
a new land with big dreams of making and selling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:20.840
chocolate, inspired by his mother, but he has no&nbsp;
money to his name. Desperate for a place to stay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:26.040
he takes a suspiciously cheap room deal from&nbsp;
Mrs. Scrubbit and Bleacher, unable to read the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:31.320
fine print forcing him into decades of indentured&nbsp;
servitude. Wonka's plans to pay off his debts by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:36.080
selling chocolate also face the roadblock&nbsp;
of the Chocolate Cartel led by Slugworth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:39.600
who are in cahoots with the chief of&nbsp;
police to shut down all competition.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:44.240
Wonka teams up with Scrubbit's other hapless&nbsp;
victims to surreptitiously build their chocolate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:48.840
empire — though they occasionally have to resupply&nbsp;
ingredients stolen by an Oompa-Loompa seeking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:54.080
payback for a previous cocoa theft. Wonka's&nbsp;
most significant friend in this group is Noodle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:58.960
an orphaned girl who teaches him how to read.&nbsp;
Wonka's magical confections prove so popular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:04.200
with the public that he's eventually able to open&nbsp;
his own store. The opening, however, is sabotaged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:09.400
by the Cartel, who pay off Scrubbit and Bleacher&nbsp;
to poison the store's supply. At his lowest point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:14.720
Wonka is given an ultimatum — if he leaves&nbsp;
town, all his friends' debts will be paid off.

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:18.880
On the boat out of town, the Oompa-Loompa&nbsp;
catches up with Wonka, encouraging him to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:23.920
fight back against the Cartel. Wonka realizes&nbsp;
that Slugworth's signet ring matches Noodle's — it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:29.040
turns out she's Slugworth's niece, and she wasn't&nbsp;
freed but instead bought by Scrubbit and Bleacher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:33.560
to keep her out of her family's fortune. Wonka&nbsp;
returns to reunite with his friends for a massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:38.560
heist to acquire the documented evidence of&nbsp;
the Cartel's crimes. They find the documents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:43.080
but the Cartel traps Wonka and Noodle in a&nbsp;
flooding chocolate chamber. The Oompa-Loompa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:47.600
still on the trail of Wonka's chocolate, ends&nbsp;
up rescuing them before they drown. The police&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:51.360
turn on their corrupt chief after seeing&nbsp;
the evidence, the Cartel leaders are blown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:56.240
away after eating Wonka's levitation candies,&nbsp;
and the chocolate explodes into a fountain.

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:00.680
Wonka opens the bar of chocolate he's been&nbsp;
saving from his mother, and inside is a golden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:05.200
ticket with a message about the joy of sharing&nbsp;
chocolate with people. Noodle's mom is found,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:09.840
allowing for a mother-daughter reunion as&nbsp;
Wonka sings "Pure Imagination," and Wonka&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:14.200
and the Oompa-Loompa make a deal to work&nbsp;
together to build their chocolate factory.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:19.040
"Wonka" spells out its ultimate moral loud and&nbsp;
clear, literally writing it on a golden ticket.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:23.200
It's the old cliche of "the real treasure was&nbsp;
the friends we made along the way," delivered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:27.320
with just enough conviction and earnestness&nbsp;
to hopefully make viewers' eyes water more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:31.560
than they roll. Wonka's motivation from the&nbsp;
beginning is driven by his love for his mother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:35.640
and though she's no longer with him except in&nbsp;
memory, he's now built up a group of friends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:39.640
and has a "found family" he seeks to take&nbsp;
care of. The movie also makes clear that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:43.640
you can balance caring for others with&nbsp;
pursuing your personal dreams — Wonka was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:48.200
ready to give up the latter for the sake of&nbsp;
the former, but he realizes he can do both.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:49.880
"What are you doing?"

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:51.320
"I'm making chocolate of course."

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:55.520
This message is perhaps the one thing the&nbsp;
movie has in common with the 2005 "Charlie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.520 --> 00:03:00.520
and the Chocolate Factory." Whereas the book&nbsp;
and the 1971 movie use Wonka primarily as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:04.800
an arbiter of sin and virtue for the kids&nbsp;
visiting the factory to learn lessons from,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:10.080
the 2005 movie shifts the arc more towards&nbsp;
Wonka himself learning the value of family.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:13.000
The difference is that Johnny Depp's&nbsp;
version of Wonka learned that lesson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:17.960
at an older age after having a much more&nbsp;
fraught relationship with his own family.

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:22.080
While the ending of "Wonka" works successfully&nbsp;
as a conclusion to the movie's self-contained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:25.800
narrative, it's weird as an origin&nbsp;
story for this particular character.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:29.360
Whether you're talking about the book&nbsp;
or the two previous movie adaptations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:33.800
Willy Wonka is a character famous for&nbsp;
his mysteriousness and reclusiveness.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:37.400
The Gene Wilder version in particular&nbsp;
has a sharp cynical edge — he doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:41.600
even feign caring about kids getting injured&nbsp;
on his factory tour, and even Charlie nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:46.240
gets screwed out of his reward due to the fine&nbsp;
print in a contract he couldn't possibly read.

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:49.920
If "Wonka" is all about a younger&nbsp;
Wonka learning the value of friendship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:54.080
isn't it weird to think he'd later go on to&nbsp;
shut himself up in his factory and shun the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:58.280
outside world? Perhaps the Oompa-Loompas would&nbsp;
be friends enough for him in his recluse years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:02.280
but surely he'd want to stay in some contact&nbsp;
with Noodle and his other human friends as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:06.760
right? Furthermore, it's sad to imagine this&nbsp;
version of Wonka, screwed over by contract law&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:10.920
in his youth, growing up to be the same man&nbsp;
who would try to do the same to other people.

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:15.080
"The suspense is terrible. I hope it'll last."

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:19.760
It's not really clear whether this is a standalone&nbsp;
story about Willy Wonka or intended to eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:24.560
segue into the events of the novel and films. Paul&nbsp;
King's film does offer other points of distinction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:29.320
that might imply this is a different continuity:&nbsp;
Where the book explicitly takes place in England&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:34.920
and the 1971 movie in the United States, "Wonka"&nbsp;
seems to take place in a fantasy setting. Their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:39.360
monetary currency of "silver sovereigns" is&nbsp;
fictional. Magic holds a greater presence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:43.600
in this world than it did in other Wonka&nbsp;
stories, especially in the musical numbers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:50.726
The period is also distinctly indistinct, with an&nbsp;
anachronistic mishmash of styles and technologies.

00:04:50.726 --> 00:04:54.680
And this film's version of Arthur Slugworth is&nbsp;
definitely a different take from the 1971 version.&nbsp;

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:58.960
In his performance, Timothée Chalamet isn't&nbsp;
attempting an impression of Gene Wilder;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:04.080
if anything, his exaggerated comic deliveries&nbsp;
and good-natured yet foolish characterization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:08.480
feel like they take more inspiration from Jim&nbsp;
Carrey's performances. If the Chalamet Wonka&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:13.000
is supposed to in fact be a younger version&nbsp;
of the Wilder Wonka, then presumably a lot is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:18.440
going to change in his life between "Wonka" and&nbsp;
whenever he gives Charlie Bucket a factory tour.

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:22.960
If the Chalamet Wonka is to eventually become&nbsp;
the Wilder Wonka, it would be at least in part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:26.520
thanks to the influence of the Hugh Grant&nbsp;
Oompa-Loompa. Though he only appears for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:30.840
brief portions of the film, the Oompa-Loompa&nbsp;
turns out to be one of its most important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:35.600
characters in terms of moving the story along.&nbsp;
His characterization is in many ways closer to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:40.160
the Wonka audiences already know and love, both&nbsp;
in his wittiness and in his extreme pursuit of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:44.960
justice that borders on sadism. By Loompa&nbsp;
law, any crime must be met with 1000 times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:49.400
the punishment — the sort of philosophy that&nbsp;
might justify turning annoying gum-chewing kids&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:53.960
into blueberries or shrinking television&nbsp;
addicts and teleporting them into a TV.

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:58.040
It's the Oompa-Loompa who encourages Wonka&nbsp;
to fight back against the Chocolate Cartel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:01.960
setting the story's climax in motion.&nbsp;
While this fighting back doesn't escalate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:05.800
into the nightmare fuel of book Wonka&nbsp;
or Wilder Wonka's ironic punishments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:10.720
it does at least push the character a bit closer&nbsp;
to classic Roald Dahl-style darkness rather than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:15.320
keeping him purely a figure of innocent whimsy.&nbsp;
Combine the Oompa-Loompa's influence with Noodle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:19.880
starting to give Wonka a more proper education&nbsp;
on non-chocolate subjects, and you can at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:24.320
imagine the roots of how this version of Wonka is&nbsp;
starting to become closer to the original in some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:28.840
ways — even if the "power of friendship"&nbsp;
stuff makes him more different in others.

00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:32.960
In an interview with Games Radar, coinciding&nbsp;
with the release of the film's trailer in July,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:37.680
Paul King gave insight into how he went about&nbsp;
approaching his conception of a younger Wonka.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:41.080
Doing so was a "challenge," he&nbsp;
said, before continuing to say,

00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:45.080
"But I also felt -- maybe misguidedly,&nbsp;
but just probably from those childhood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:47.720
readings — that I did know who this person was."

00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:51.280
"How do you like it? Dark,&nbsp;
white, nutty, absolutely insane?"

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:54.600
He pointed to the end of "Willy Wonka &amp;&nbsp;
the Chocolate Factory" as proof of how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:58.640
even the aged, cynical version of the&nbsp;
character still believed in "goodness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:02.240
kindness, and hope," which was key&nbsp;
to developing his younger version.

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:06.000
Noodle, he explained, was necessary&nbsp;
as a "slightly more cynical person"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:09.720
to counterbalance young Wonka's&nbsp;
innocence. Even so, King acknowledged,

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:12.440
"I'm not sure my soul is quite&nbsp;
as dark as Roald Dahl's."

00:07:12.440 --> 00:07:14.680
That sentiment goes some way to explaining why the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:19.320
film ends on such a happy note with&nbsp;
Wonka's positivity generally intact.

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:22.720
Hugh Grant's job, both in performing&nbsp;
in and promoting Paul King's films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:27.120
has been to bring his trademark cantankerousness&nbsp;
to a role that could otherwise risk becoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:32.320
sickly sweet. Speaking to the Associated Press&nbsp;
alongside Timothée Chalamet, Grant took shots at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:36.960
the director's perfectionism and the strangeness&nbsp;
of playing an Oompa-Loompa, while still making it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:41.080
clear he loved being part of the production.&nbsp;
Talking about the film's moral, he said,

00:07:41.080 --> 00:07:44.400
"It's not a trite, tacked-on&nbsp;
motto. It comes from his heart."

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:47.680
Chalamet seconded this assessment&nbsp;
that the director really means it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:52.120
while acknowledging he'd be "suspicious" if he&nbsp;
heard about it from the outside. Chalamet compared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:56.520
the experience to his work on Greta Gerwig's&nbsp;
"Little Women" in terms of being an adaptation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.520 --> 00:08:02.000
of a classic story that's done well enough&nbsp;
to overcome skepticism about IP-based movies.

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:04.920
"This movie feels like a breath of&nbsp;
fresh air with a story that's outside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:09.000
the bounds of Roald Dahl's original&nbsp;
material. I thought it was magical."

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:13.400
In a Total Film article, King revealed he&nbsp;
was open to making sequels to "Wonka." He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:17.200
pointed out how Roald Dahl, a writer who&nbsp;
"didn't really write sequels," nonetheless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:20.560
kept exploring different things he could&nbsp;
do with the character of Willy Wonka. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:24.880
wrote "Charlie and the Great Glass Elevator,"&nbsp;
featuring Wonka, and even had plans to write a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:29.280
third book in the series, so there's plenty&nbsp;
of potential for continuation. King said,

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:32.840
"There's an awful lot more Wonka story that&nbsp;
we have that we would like to tell. It's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.840 --> 00:08:37.240
like 'Dune: Part One' where you go, 'This is&nbsp;
what's happening in Part Two.' Hopefully it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.240 --> 00:08:41.280
works exquisitely as a stand-alone movie.&nbsp;
But I would definitely like to do more.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:44.916
And I'd like to spend more time in this&nbsp;
world, and meet some more Oompa Loompas."

